            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "pose builds a whole pose out of single joints. It has one inlet per joint, and whenever any of them receives a rotation, the whole pose goes out.\n\nTHE NODES:\n\npose         20 joint inlets in, a 20-joint pose out\nshadow_pose  the same 20 inlets, but the pose out has the 37 rows of the\n             raw Shadow layout\n\nWHAT A POSE IS:\nAn array of joint orientations, one row per joint, each a quaternion w x y z with the real part first. pose sends the 20 active joints in the order active_joints uses: base of skull, upper vertebrae, mid vertebrae, lower vertebrae, spine pelvis, pelvis anchor, then left hip, knee and ankle, right hip, knee and ankle, left shoulder blade, shoulder, elbow and wrist, and the same four on the right. So a pose made here can go anywhere a reduced suit pose goes.\n\nshadow_pose puts each of the same 20 inlets at its row in the 37-row layout the shadow node sends. The 17 rows without an inlet are always the identity.\n\nJOINTS YOU DO NOT SET:\nAn inlet that has received nothing counts as the identity - no rotation - and an inlet keeps the last rotation it received. So you can drive two or three joints and leave the rest of the body at rest.\n\nWHAT AN INLET TAKES:\nFour numbers, w x y z: a numpy array, a torch tensor, or a plain list - from a message box, say. euler_to_quaternion turns angles into one, as the example does.\n\nSYNTAX:\npose\nshadow_pose\n\nEXAMPLE:\npose\n\nINPUTS:\n\nbase of skull ... right wrist:\nOne quaternion each, w x y z. Any of them arriving sends the pose.\n\nOUTPUT:\n\npose out:\nThe pose as a numpy array: 20 rows of four for pose, 37 rows of four for shadow_pose.\n\nRELATED:\nactive_pose shows a whole pose row by row, and lets you edit it. active_joints reduces a 37-row Shadow pose to these 20 joints. gl_body draws a pose of either size.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 810,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 870,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "pose builds a whole pose out of single joints. It has one inlet per joint, and whenever any of them receives a rotation, the whole pose goes out.\n\nTHE NODES:\n\npose         20 joint inlets in, a 20-joint pose out\nshadow_pose  the same 20 inlets, but the pose out has the 37 rows of the\n             raw Shadow layout\n\nWHAT A POSE IS:\nAn array of joint orientations, one row per joint, each a quaternion w x y z with the real part first. pose sends the 20 active joints in the order active_joints uses: base of skull, upper vertebrae, mid vertebrae, lower vertebrae, spine pelvis, pelvis anchor, then left hip, knee and ankle, right hip, knee and ankle, left shoulder blade, shoulder, elbow and wrist, and the same four on the right. So a pose made here can go anywhere a reduced suit pose goes.\n\nshadow_pose puts each of the same 20 inlets at its row in the 37-row layout the shadow node sends. The 17 rows without an inlet are always the identity.\n\nJOINTS YOU DO NOT SET:\nAn inlet that has received nothing counts as the identity - no rotation - and an inlet keeps the last rotation it received. So you can drive two or three joints and leave the rest of the body at rest.\n\nWHAT AN INLET TAKES:\nFour numbers, w x y z: a numpy array, a torch tensor, or a plain list - from a message box, say. euler_to_quaternion turns angles into one, as the example does.\n\nSYNTAX:\npose\nshadow_pose\n\nEXAMPLE:\npose\n\nINPUTS:\n\nbase of skull ... right wrist:\nOne quaternion each, w x y z. Any of them arriving sends the pose.\n\nOUTPUT:\n\npose out:\nThe pose as a numpy array: 20 rows of four for pose, 37 rows of four for shadow_pose.\n\nRELATED:\nactive_pose shows a whole pose row by row, and lets you edit it. active_joints reduces a 37-row Shadow pose to these 20 joints. gl_body draws a pose of either size."
        },
